WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.594
From Peking University and Google,

00:00:02.674 --> 00:00:06.815
CITransNet is a context-integrated transformer for
auction design.

00:00:06.895 --> 00:00:10.843
It maximizes seller revenue while staying
incentive-compatible,

00:00:10.923 --> 00:00:12.825
discovers asymmetric solutions,

00:00:12.905 --> 00:00:16.790
and generalizes to auction sizes it never saw
during training.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:22.656
A classic problem in auction theory is designing a
mechanism that maximizes the seller's

00:00:22.736 --> 00:00:25.375
revenue while staying incentive-compatible.

00:00:25.455 --> 00:00:30.562
Myerson solved the single-item case in 1981, but
the multi-item case remains open,

00:00:30.642 --> 00:00:34.230
and prior deep methods assume fixed or symmetric auctions.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.162
Real auctions are richer.

00:00:36.242 --> 00:00:37.860
In e-commerce advertising,

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.842
many bidders compete for many ad slots described by
features,

00:00:41.922 --> 00:00:45.433
and every round has a different number of participants.

00:00:45.513 --> 00:00:50.590
We need an architecture that absorbs context and
handles varying auction sizes.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.106
The paper makes two contributions.

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:59.492
It extends the RegretNet framework to the
contextual setting and proves a sample-complexity bound.

00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:04.510
And it introduces CITransNet,
permutation-equivariant over bids and contexts,

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.921
not restricted to symmetric auctions,

00:01:07.001 --> 00:01:10.310
with a parameter count independent of auction scale.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:14.193
CITransNet takes three inputs: the bidding profile,
bidder-contexts,

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:17.776
and item-contexts.

00:01:17.856 --> 00:01:21.359
It embeds contexts with bids into a representation
per bidder-item pair,

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:24.941
then passes it through transformer interaction
layers that mix a global average with row and column transformers,

00:01:25.021 --> 00:01:28.524
staying permutation-equivariant.

00:01:28.604 --> 00:01:32.107
A final layer outputs allocations and payments,

00:01:32.187 --> 00:01:35.690
with zero-regret enforced during training.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.736
They evaluate on nine synthetic settings.

00:01:38.816 --> 00:01:41.214
Three single-item cases, A through C,

00:01:41.294 --> 00:01:44.898
have known Myerson-optimal solutions as a sanity check.

00:01:44.978 --> 00:01:50.591
The other six, D through I, are multi-item auctions
up to five bidders and ten items,

00:01:50.671 --> 00:01:53.940
the largest size in the auction-design literature.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.164
Results come in two parts.

00:01:56.244 --> 00:02:01.938
On single-item settings, CITransNet nearly recovers
Myerson's optimal revenue,

00:02:02.018 --> 00:02:05.935
0.593 against 0.594, with regret below one thousandth,

00:02:06.015 --> 00:02:08.970
while context-blind baselines fall short.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.486
On the harder multi-item settings,

00:02:11.566 --> 00:02:16.371
it beats the Item-wise Myerson baseline in all six
configurations,

00:02:16.451 --> 00:02:18.740
with gains reaching ten percent.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.618
To isolate why it works,

00:02:20.698 --> 00:02:28.244
the authors replace the transformer layers with
RegretNet's fully-connected and EquivariantNet's equivariant layers,

00:02:28.324 --> 00:02:30.874
giving CIRegretNet and CIEquivariantNet.

00:02:30.954 --> 00:02:34.622
Both still get context, yet earn less revenue everywhere,

00:02:34.702 --> 00:02:37.449
pinning the gain on the transformer layers.

00:02:37.529 --> 00:02:40.210
Removing context hurts even easy settings.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:43.333
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:02:43.413 --> 00:02:50.176
On the single-item benchmark, CITransNet reaches
0.593 versus the optimal 0.594.

00:02:50.256 --> 00:02:57.532
In the three-bidder ten-item setting it earns 6.872
against Item-wise Myerson's 6.509,

00:02:57.612 --> 00:03:01.981
and one continuous-context margin nears ten percent.

00:03:02.061 --> 00:03:05.060
It wins all six multi-item settings.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:10.531
The takeaway: context as a first-class input
unlocks better learned auctions.

00:03:10.611 --> 00:03:15.702
CITransNet is near-optimal, asymmetric when needed,
and incentive-compatible.

00:03:15.782 --> 00:03:22.350
Its size-independent parameter count lets one
trained model transfer to auction sizes it never saw.
